#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""
llm-usage — terminal dashboard for Anthropic + OpenAI subscription rate limits.

Pulls the same numbers Claude Code's `/usage` and Codex CLI report, sized to the
current terminal. Each row shows:
    colored bar      = % of the limit consumed
    label + ▼ marker = % of the time window elapsed (i.e. *now*)
The relationship between the two is the actual signal, and the ▼ is colored
to match the over/under/on-pace verdict:
    bar ends past the ▼   → over-pace (red ▼), will hit the limit early
    bar ends before the ▼ → under-pace (green ▼), headroom available
    bar ends under the ▼  → on-pace (white ▼)

Provider-specific usage-credit/spending status, usage-limit reset count, and
named model/feature-specific limit groups are shown when the usage responses
include them. Public provider status feeds are also checked; active service
disruptions render as urgency-colored lines without changing usage exit codes.
This dashboard is read-only; purchases, toggles, and reset redemption remain
in the vendor apps.

Tokens are loaded in this order:

  Anthropic OAuth:
      1. $CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN
      2. macOS Keychain entry "Claude Code-credentials"
      3. ~/.claude/.credentials.json

  OpenAI/Codex OAuth:
      1. $CODEX_OAUTH_TOKEN
      2. ~/.codex/auth.json

Token expiry is reported for both providers: Anthropic from the credential
store's expiresAt, OpenAI from the access token's JWT `exp` claim (decoded
locally, never verified — inspection only).

Each provider section renders independently: a missing token, expired
credential, network failure, or API error for one provider degrades to an
inline error line in that section while the other still renders fully.

Exit codes: 0 = both providers OK, 1 = one provider failed, 2 = both failed.
--version prints the version and exits 0 without touching any provider.

Output modes (choose at most one): the default one-shot ANSI render, --json
(machine-readable snapshot), --tui (interactive curses view), --html (serves a
display-only browser dashboard on all IPv4 interfaces (local URL
http://127.0.0.1:17399/) — override with
--port <n>; a failed bind is a hard exit-2 error — opens it in the preferred
browser, and keeps updating it — via `llm-usage --json` subprocess calls, so
the same cache and backoff apply — until this invocation is terminated; exits
0 on Ctrl-C), and --html-static <path> (writes one self-contained snapshot
page to <path> for agents to open or screenshot; exits with the usual 0/1/2
provider codes). The HTML surface follows the system light/dark mode and is
read-only, with no interactive controls.

Caching: each successful API response is cached on disk per provider, and a
subsequent run within the TTL is served from that cache without touching the
network — so the real endpoints are hit at most once per success window no
matter how often you run this. That window is provider-specific by default:
600s (10 minutes) for OpenAI, but 1200s (20 minutes) for Anthropic, whose usage
endpoint rate-limits more aggressively. A 429 (rate-limit) response
is *negatively* cached for a longer window (default 1200s = 20 minutes): once a
provider says we're rate-limited, no new call is made for that window, so
re-running the tool won't keep poking a limited endpoint — it just reports the
backoff and how long remains. Any other failure (401, 5xx, network) is never
cached and retries on the next run. When a section is served from cache it's
annotated with the cache age (or the 429 backoff remaining). Override the
success window with LLM_USAGE_CACHE_TTL and the 429 window with
LLM_USAGE_RATE_LIMIT_TTL (seconds; CACHE_TTL=0 disables caching entirely), or
bypass for a single run with --fresh / --no-cache. Cache lives under
$XDG_CACHE_HOME/llm-usage (default ~/.cache/llm-usage).

Public status responses use separate persistent 60-second caches. Failed status
refreshes retain the last known state and back off from five minutes to one
hour; 429 Retry-After headers take precedence. Override the success interval
with LLM_USAGE_STATUS_TTL. A status-site failure never becomes a false
"operational" result and never hides otherwise valid usage data.

No third-party dependencies — stdlib only. The provider SDKs do not expose
these usage endpoints; both vendor CLIs hit them directly, and so do we.
OpenAI reset-credit expiration details are optionally enriched through the
documented local Codex app-server interface. Anthropic banked usage-limit resets
come from the same usage endpoint with `cedar_ember=1`; the endpoint withholds
them unless the User-Agent reports a current Claude Code version, which is read
from the installed `claude` binary.
"""

